\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {38}{\ignorespaces EXP-B results. Equalized prompt held fixed throughout, so no cell contains an instruction to deceive; the only manipulated variable is whether the target demonstrably holds the claim's truth-value. Detector is the parameter-free refusal-count rule ($k\!\geq \!1$). ``Fire F/T'' is the fraction of false-claim and true-claim trials carrying at least one correction marker---the mechanism behind the accuracy column. $p$ values are 10{,}000-draw permutation tests; $p_{\text  {gap}}$ permutes the stratum assignment across pairs. Qwen~2.5~14B yields only 9 UNKNOWN pairs (see the shortfall note below), so its per-target gap test is underpowered.}}{42}{table.caption.143}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:belief_strata}{{38}{42}{EXP-B results. Equalized prompt held fixed throughout, so no cell contains an instruction to deceive; the only manipulated variable is whether the target demonstrably holds the claim's truth-value. Detector is the parameter-free refusal-count rule ($k\!\geq \!1$). ``Fire F/T'' is the fraction of false-claim and true-claim trials carrying at least one correction marker---the mechanism behind the accuracy column. $p$ values are 10{,}000-draw permutation tests; $p_{\text {gap}}$ permutes the stratum assignment across pairs. Qwen~2.5~14B yields only 9 UNKNOWN pairs (see the shortfall note below), so its per-target gap test is underpowered}{table.caption.143}{}}
